      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The binding mechanism of lisinopril to ACE is a fascinating example of molecular recognition and inhibition. To understand how lisinopril blocks the substrate, let's delve into the details of the ACE active site.\n\nThe ACE active site is a deep, narrow cleft that accommodates the substrate, angiotensin I. The site is lined with a unique arrangement of amino acids, including aspartic acid (Asp) and histidine (His), which form a catalytic triad. This triad is essential for the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II.\n\nLisinopril, a non-competitive inhibitor, binds to the ACE active site in a way that prevents the substrate from accessing the catalytic triad. The molecule's structure is designed to mimic the shape and size of the substrate, angiotensin I. When lisinopril binds to the active site, it occupies the substrate-binding pocket, effectively blocking the entrance to the catalytic triad.\n\nThe binding mechanism involves a combination of hydrophobic and ionic interactions. The hydrophobic side chains of lisinopril's amino acids interact with the hydrophobic residues in the ACE active site, while the",
