    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, so it's basically a physical blockade. it sounds almost like a \"gatekeeper\" situation where if the inhibitor is in the way, nothing else can get through to the enzyme. could you explain why that block is so effective? like, why does the enzyme not just kick the lisinopril out easily and keep working?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The blockade is indeed a physical one, and it's a testament to the clever design of lisinopril that it's so effective. The reason why ACE doesn't simply \"kick\" the inhibitor out is due to the unique properties of the ACE active site and the binding mechanism.\n\nWhen lisinopril binds to the active site, it forms a stable complex with the enzyme. This complex is stabilized by a combination of hydrophobic, ionic, and hydrogen bonding interactions between the inhibitor and the enzyme. The binding is also influenced by the conformational changes that occur in the enzyme upon binding, which further stabilize the complex.\n\nOne key factor that contributes to the stability of the complex is the presence of a \"gatekeeper\" residue, a histidine residue (His) in the ACE active site. This residue plays a crucial role in stabilizing the inhibitor and preventing it from being easily displaced by the substrate.\n\nWhen the substrate, angiotensin I, tries to bind to the active site, it encounters the bound lisinopril. The substrate is unable to bind to the enzyme because the inhibitor is occupying the substrate-binding pocket, effectively blocking the entrance to the catalytic triad. This is known as a \"competitive inhibition",
      "source": "target",
